https://solodit.cyfrin.io/issues/due-dil.audit_content:
Vulnerabilities in NFT Locking Mechanism
It is possible for a farm manager to modify the locking duration, preventing users from properly unlocking NFTs. This can be abused to allow the farm manager to later steal locked NFTs.
Code Snippet (Rust)
----new code block----
// Unstake NFT twice
let gemworks_farm_authority_bump = 
    *ctx.bumps.get("gemworks_farm_authority").unwrap();
let gemworks_farm_treasury_bump = 
    *ctx.bumps.get("gemworks_farm_treasury").unwrap();
let gemworks_farmer_bump = 
    *ctx.bumps.get("gemworks_farmer").unwrap();
​
let unstake_ctx = CpiContext::new_with_signer(
    ctx.accounts.gemfarm_program.to_account_info().clone(),
    Unstake {
        ...
    },
    farmer_authority_signer_seeds,
);
​
unstake(unstake_ctx, ...)?;

let unstake_again_ctx = CpiContext::new_with_signer(
    ctx.accounts.gemfarm_program.to_account_info().clone(),
    Unstake {
        ...
    },
    farmer_authority_signer_seeds,
);
​
unstake(unstake_again_ctx, ...)?;
----end of code block ----
Unstaking twice in a row (immediately unstaking again after unstaking by taking cooldown sec as 0) leads to a potential DoS condition, since the
cooldown_period_sec
and
min_staking_sec
parameters are under the control of the farm manager.
© 2022 OtterSec LLC. All Rights Reserved.
Validation Issues
The
validate_farm
function in
stake_nft
is not sufficient to ensure that
min_cooldown_sec
is 0. This is because
min_staking_sec
is only taken from the current farm configuration, whereas
cooldown_period_sec
is taken from the updated farm configuration at the time of unstaking.
As a result, if
cooldown_period_sec
is updated to a non-zero value, the unstake won’t occur in solvent. This may even happen unintentionally when updating the farm’s configuration, which may lead to unexpected runtime errors and denial of service.
Proof of Concept
To reproduce the exploit, follow the steps below:
The solvent-admin for a bucket adds the farm details to the staking parameters.
User A locks their NFT, specifying a 100-minute duration and taking a principal amount.
The farm manager stakes the NFT locked by user A.
The farm manager updates the
cooldown_period_sec
to 10.
Since unstake isn’t possible in the solvent-contract, the NFT is locked in the farm. This means that user A can’t unlock their NFT before the specified duration (100 minutes) has passed.
After waiting the specified duration, the farm manager can execute a transaction as follows to get the NFT:
(a) Update
cooldown_period_sec
to 0 in the farm configuration.
(b) Execute the
unstake_nft
instruction of the solvent-contract.
(c) Liquidate the NFT that was locked by user A, as the specified lock duration has passed and the NFT is now unstaked. This will send the NFT to the public bucket.
(d) The farm manager can now redeem the NFT for 100 droplet tokens.
Remediation
Since the farm configuration can be tampered with, it is recommended to keep the unstake and cooldown operations of a gem farm restricted to two different instructions. Another possible solution is adding a check for
cooldown_period_sec
at the second unstake CPI in the
unstake_nft
instruction.
Patch
Modified the
unstake_nft
to handle
end_staking
,
end_cooldown
in two separate instruction invocations, similar to the unstake instruction in gemfarm. Fixed in #108.
----new code block----
// If NFT is staked: unstake once and put it into cooldown period
if farmer.state == FarmerState::Staked {
 // ....
}
​
// If cooldown is over and the second unstake is due: unstake,
else if farmer.state == FarmerState::PendingCooldown && clock.unix_timestamp as u64 >= farmer.cooldown_ends_ts {
 // ...
}
​
// If cooldown is still pending, return an error
else if farmer.state == FarmerState::PendingCooldown {
 return err!(SolventError::StakingCooldownPending);
}
----end of code block ----
